    "post_rendered": "<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\"  width=\"57\" height=\"13\" > is a convex quadrilateral. We draw its diagnals to divide the quadrilateral to four triabgles. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > is the intersection of diagnals. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/8/1389440769baa8d1928038ec164e09c5305f3463.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},I_{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"16\" > are excenters of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/0/7a09c73ddeb10199d20825e20977cdcb21306c96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PAD,PAB,PBC,PCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"196\" height=\"16\" >(</span>excenters corresponding vertex <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" >)</span>. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/8/1389440769baa8d1928038ec164e09c5305f3463.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},I_{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"16\" > lie on a circle iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\"  width=\"57\" height=\"13\" > is a tangential quadrilateral.",
    "post_canonical": "$ABCD$ is a convex quadrilateral. We draw its diagnals to divide the quadrilateral to four triabgles. $P$ is the intersection of diagnals. $I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},I_{4}$ are excenters of $PAD,PAB,PBC,PCD$(excenters corresponding vertex $P$). Prove that $I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},I_{4}$ lie on a circle iff $ABCD$ is a tangential quadrilateral.",
