    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 112346,
    "poster_id": 652,
    "post_rendered": "Assume <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/6/046071ac96f220896504256b73d2df2acd820aed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{1},x_{2},\\dots,x_{n}\\in\\mathbb R^{+}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"148\" height=\"18\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/c/cecd42c6aac8b4fb379fbf1fde18f080ef20cb16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"81\" height=\"48\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/4/7044fec967a31f925fb395380f7576b8cc0d50e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\\geq s&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"110\" height=\"48\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/2/6b2a33a3b1f63504506c6c20ace8b8c7cf2bb3e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\leq\\lambda\\leq1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" >.</span> Prove that at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4cff95149680d4198ddff2df22e8a616fb7fbfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lceil\\frac{s^{2}(1-\\lambda)^{2}}n\\right\\rceil$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"100\" height=\"43\" > of these numbers are larger than <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/5/1454d1f9e5f4df89431eca7f4025665671f85b5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\lambda s}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"21\" height=\"38\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Assume $x_{1},x_{2},\\dots,x_{n}\\in\\mathbb R^{+}$, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}=n$, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\\geq s>0$ and $0\\leq\\lambda\\leq1$. Prove that at least $\\left\\lceil\\frac{s^{2}(1-\\lambda)^{2}}n\\right\\rceil$ of these numbers are larger than $\\frac{\\lambda s}{n}$.",
    "username": "Omid Hatami",
