  {
   "item_id": 7970,
   "item_text": "12",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "986",
   "id": "33496",
   "item_level": "986",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 7970,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 2628,
    "poster_id": 26,
    "post_rendered": "We call a permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/d/aade142d24b247784ad30d61607c23a5c1d1b340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\left(a_1, a_2, ..., a_n\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"18\" > of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/a/f6a7eef23468adea2b9aca0293592894015dd1de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\left(1, 2, ..., n\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" > <i>quadratic</i> if there exists at least a perfect square among the numbers <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/7/de7b0c6ea3e31c387633fa07b2b2c195c01d58e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/8/8c8bf5690c8f4baa2af9f14b64fc3253105c4cde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_1 + a_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/8/3788c359110813af7c92f7274ef0f42fbb0a0213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ ...$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"2\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/3/7730825babb8d3e39ed83814c36ca01eac154e02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_1 + a_2 + ... + a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"133\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Find all natural numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d3f8b726378d5420223c5cb14b10f24b202b187.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > such that all permutations in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/d/fadf331ef65921140f0d8aaeefdd0c08c9b2926d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ S_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" > are quadratic.<br>\n<br>\n<i>Remark.</i> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/3/3f3c55dad3c679a5ae61c4f564e51cdba922c985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ S_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" > denotes the <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d3f8b726378d5420223c5cb14b10f24b202b187.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >-</span>th symmetric group, the group of permutations on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d3f8b726378d5420223c5cb14b10f24b202b187.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > elements.",
    "post_canonical": "We call a permutation $ \\left(a_1, a_2, ..., a_n\\right)$ of $ \\left(1, 2, ..., n\\right)$ [i]quadratic[/i] if there exists at least a perfect square among the numbers $ a_1$, $ a_1 \\plus{} a_2$, $ ...$, $ a_1 \\plus{} a_2 \\plus{} ... \\plus{} a_n$. Find all natural numbers $ n$ such that all permutations in $ S_n$ are quadratic.\r\n\r\n[i]Remark.[/i] $ S_{n}$ denotes the $ n$-th symmetric group, the group of permutations on $ n$ elements.",
    "username": "grobber",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/no-avatar.png"
   }
