   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 7954,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 2619,
    "poster_id": 26,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > be a triangle. The incircle of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > touches the side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > at <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/b/e6b1b9c93d04970ce621b9ca7680f944601d6666.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^{\\prime}$\"  width=\"17\" height=\"14\" >,</span> and the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/3/2537916f1d1c33c6d1f779e7c54e5f24de1d79fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AA^{\\prime}$\"  width=\"30\" height=\"14\" > meets the incircle again at a point <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" >.</span> Let the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/3/d13fd509f3062449aaea6e10e1040b1d585063f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CP$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/7/f874b39870586f0504f50a667f2bafedddf47904.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BP$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > meet the incircle of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > again at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" >,</span> respectively. Prove that the lines <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/3/2537916f1d1c33c6d1f779e7c54e5f24de1d79fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AA^{\\prime}$\"  width=\"30\" height=\"14\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0fa81c0cbf8b517733059abb0faf839dd2a56300.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BN$\"  width=\"31\" height=\"12\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/3/fa38fd42cdaa5db0dd4e0f7df98ae79a598eb627.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CM$\"  width=\"33\" height=\"12\" > are concurrent.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $ABC$ be a triangle. The incircle of triangle $ABC$ touches the side $BC$ at $A^{\\prime}$, and the line $AA^{\\prime}$ meets the incircle again at a point $P$. Let the lines $CP$ and $BP$ meet the incircle of triangle $ABC$ again at $N$ and $M$, respectively. Prove that the lines $AA^{\\prime}$, $BN$ and $CM$ are concurrent.",
    "username": "grobber",
    "post_number": 1,
